# --- part 1: Etruscans, Aegean scripts, Egypt and Rome
E+=[
X('etruscan-origins','Where did the Etruscans come from?',H,'disputed',2,-900,['textual','archaeological','genetic (ancient DNA)','linguistic'],
 "The Etruscans dominated central Italy from roughly the 9th century BCE until they were absorbed by Rome, speaking a non-Indo-European language that is readable (it uses a Greek-derived alphabet) but still only partly understood. Ancient writers disagreed about their origin: Herodotus said they came from Lydia in Anatolia, while Dionysius of Halicarnassus said they were native to Italy. Most archaeologists trace Etruscan culture to the local Villanovan culture, and a 2021 ancient-DNA study (Posth et al., Science Advances) found that Etruscans were genetically similar to their Latin neighbours, with steppe-related ancestry and no sign of a recent Anatolian migration.",
 [('Lydian migration','The Herodotus account of a migration from Lydia was long popular and some earlier modern DNA studies of cattle and present-day Tuscans were read as supporting an eastern link; the 2021 ancient-DNA results weigh against this.'),
  ('Language puzzle','Because Etruscan is related only to the scarcely attested Raetic and Lemnian languages, some researchers propose an Aegean or Alpine connection for the language even if the population was largely local.')],
 ['wiki:Etruscan_origins','doi:10.1126/sciadv.abi7673','wiki:Etruscan_civilization'],img='Etruscan_civilization',
 changed="Debated since antiquity; archaeology from the 20th century favoured local origins, and ancient DNA in 2021 found no recent Anatolian ancestry."),
X('liber-linteus','Liber Linteus: an Etruscan book wrapped around an Egyptian mummy',H,'confirmed',3,-250,['textual','archaeological'],
 "The Liber Linteus Zagrabiensis is the longest surviving Etruscan text and the only known surviving linen book from antiquity. It was cut into strips and used to wrap a female mummy in Ptolemaic Egypt; a Croatian official bought the mummy in Alexandria in 1848 and it is now in the Archaeological Museum in Zagreb. Only in 1891-92 was the writing identified as Etruscan. Its roughly 1,200 legible words appear to form a ritual calendar listing ceremonies and offerings to gods, dated to around the 3rd century BCE.",
 [('Why in Egypt','How an Etruscan religious book ended up as mummy wrappings is unknown; suggestions include an Etruscan community in Egypt or linen simply being reused, and none is proven.')],
 ['wiki:Liber_Linteus'],img='Liber_Linteus'),
X('pyrgi-tablets','Pyrgi Tablets: an Etruscan-Phoenician bilingual',H,'confirmed',3,-500,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Three gold sheets found in 1964 at Pyrgi, the harbour sanctuary of the Etruscan city of Caere, record a dedication by the ruler Thefarie Velianas to the goddess Uni, identified with the Phoenician Astarte, around 500 BCE. Two are in Etruscan and one in Phoenician, making them a partial bilingual text that has helped scholars understand Etruscan vocabulary, though the two versions are not word-for-word translations. They also show close Etruscan-Carthaginian ties at the time.",
 [('Interpretation','Scholars debate whether the dedication implies Phoenician/Carthaginian political influence over Caere or simply religious and trade links.')],
 ['wiki:Pyrgi_Tablets'],img='Pyrgi_Tablets'),
X('phaistos-disc','Phaistos Disc',H,'disputed',2,-1700,['archaeological','textual'],
 "A fired clay disc about 16 cm across, found in 1908 by Luigi Pernier in the Minoan palace of Phaistos on Crete, is stamped on both sides with 241 impressions of 45 different signs arranged in a spiral, made with individual punches, an early form of movable-type printing. It is usually dated to the Middle or Late Minoan Bronze Age (roughly 1850-1600 BCE). The script matches no other known find, and with so short a text it has not been deciphered; most specialists think it cannot be without further examples.",
 [('Proposed decipherments','Many readings have been published, as Greek, Luwian, Minoan prayers, a calendar or a game board; none has gained scholarly acceptance.'),
  ('Forgery suggestion','A minority view (e.g. Jerome Eisenberg, 2008) argues the disc is a modern forgery; this is not widely accepted and the museum has not permitted thermoluminescence testing.')],
 ['wiki:Phaistos_Disc'],img='Phaistos_Disc'),
X('linear-a','Linear A: the undeciphered Minoan script',H,'confirmed',2,-1800,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Linear A was used by the Minoans of Crete from about 1800 to 1450 BCE, mostly on clay tablets recording goods. Its successor Linear B was deciphered by Michael Ventris in 1952 as an early form of Greek, so many Linear A signs can be given approximate sound values, but the resulting words do not match Greek or any known language and the script remains undeciphered.",
 [('Language family','Proposals link the underlying Minoan language to Luwian/Anatolian, Semitic, Indo-Iranian or Tyrsenian (Etruscan-related) languages; none is generally accepted.')],
 ['wiki:Linear_A'],img='Linear_A'),
X('tutankhamun-tomb',"Tutankhamun's tomb and the 'curse'",H,'confirmed',1,-1323,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Howard Carter, funded by Lord Carnarvon, found the almost intact tomb KV62 of the boy king Tutankhamun (died c. 1323 BCE) in the Valley of the Kings in November 1922. It held over 5,000 objects, including the gold mask. Carnarvon's death in April 1923 from an infected mosquito bite fuelled press stories of a 'curse'; studies of the people present at the opening found no unusual death rates. CT scans and DNA work (2010) examined his health and family, suggesting he was a son of Akhenaten.",
 [('Hidden chambers','In 2015 Nicholas Reeves proposed hidden chambers behind the tomb walls, possibly the tomb of Nefertiti; radar surveys gave conflicting results and the idea remains unconfirmed.'),
  ('Curse','The curse story is treated by historians as a media creation; some writers have suggested mould or bacteria in sealed tombs could cause illness, which is speculative for this case.')],
 ['wiki:Tutankhamun','wiki:KV62','doi:10.1001/jama.2010.121'],img='Tutankhamun',
 changed="Found 1922; curse stories 1923; DNA and CT study 2010; hidden-chamber debate since 2015."),
X('sea-peoples','The Sea Peoples',H,'disputed',2,-1200,['textual','archaeological'],
 "Egyptian records, above all the reliefs of Ramesses III at Medinet Habu (c. 1175 BCE), describe a confederation of seafaring invaders, including the Peleset, Sherden, Shekelesh and Denyen, who attacked Egypt and the eastern Mediterranean at the end of the Bronze Age. The modern term 'Sea Peoples' dates from the 19th century. The Peleset are widely identified with the Philistines, whose settlement in southern Canaan is supported by ancient DNA showing a European-related component (Feldman et al., 2019). Their role in the wider Bronze Age collapse is debated.",
 [('Cause or symptom','Some historians see the Sea Peoples as the main cause of the collapse; others see them as displaced people fleeing drought, famine, earthquakes and systems failure.'),
  ('Origins','Homelands proposed include the Aegean, western Anatolia, Sardinia (Sherden) and Sicily (Shekelesh), based on name similarities that are not proven.')],
 ['wiki:Sea_Peoples','doi:10.1126/sciadv.aax0061'],img='Sea_Peoples'),
X('nemi-ships',"Caligula's Nemi ships",H,'confirmed',3,40,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Two huge ships, about 70 and 73 metres long, were built on the small volcanic Lake Nemi near Rome for the emperor Caligula (reigned 37-41 CE), apparently as floating palaces and temples. Legends of the sunken ships persisted for centuries and divers recovered pieces from the 15th century onwards. Mussolini's government drained the lake in 1928-32 to recover them, revealing advanced features such as bilge pumps and rotating platforms on bearings. They were displayed in a purpose-built museum but burned in 1944 during the Second World War; who set the fire is disputed.",
 [('The 1944 fire','An Italian commission blamed retreating German troops; others have suggested American shelling or local looters. It has never been settled.')],
 ['wiki:Nemi_ships'],img='Nemi_ships'),
X('talos-automaton','Talos, the bronze giant of Crete',M,'myth-legend',3,-700,['textual','iconographic'],
 "In Greek myth Talos was a giant bronze man made by Hephaestus (or given to Minos by Zeus) who circled Crete three times a day, throwing rocks at ships. He had a single vein closed by a bronze nail; in Apollonius of Rhodes' Argonautica (3rd century BCE) Medea brings about his death and the ichor drains out. He is often cited as one of the earliest imagined robots or automata, and appears on a 5th-century BCE vase, the Talos Vase.",
 [('Ancient technology readings','Classicist Adrienne Mayor (Gods and Robots, 2018) explores Talos as evidence that ancient Greeks imagined artificial life and automata; claims that the myth preserves memories of real ancient robots are speculative and not supported.')],
 ['wiki:Talos'],img='Talos'),
X('herculaneum-papyri','Herculaneum papyri and the Vesuvius Challenge',H,'confirmed',2,79,['archaeological','textual','computational imaging'],
 "About 1,800 carbonised papyrus scrolls were found from 1752 in the Villa of the Papyri at Herculaneum, buried by Vesuvius in 79 CE; it is the only intact library known from the classical world. Many were damaged by early attempts to unroll them. Most of those read so far are Epicurean works, many by Philodemus. From 2015, X-ray tomography and later machine learning allowed unopened scrolls to be read virtually; in 2023-24 the Vesuvius Challenge prize was won by students who recovered passages from a sealed scroll, apparently by Philodemus on pleasure. In 2025 researchers reported reading the title of another scroll.",
 [('Unexcavated library','Parts of the villa remain unexcavated and some scholars hope more scrolls, perhaps including lost Latin or Greek works, are still buried; this is unproven.')],
 ['wiki:Herculaneum_papyri','wiki:Vesuvius_Challenge','wiki:Villa_of_the_Papyri'],img='Herculaneum_papyri',
 changed="Found 1752; destructive unrolling 18th-19th c.; virtual unrolling from 2015; Vesuvius Challenge readings 2023-25."),
]
# --- part 2: Russia, the Arctic and the North Pacific coast
E+=[
X('sungir-burials','Sungir: Ice Age burials with thousands of beads',H,'confirmed',2,-32000,['archaeological','radiocarbon','genetic (ancient DNA)'],
 "Sungir, near Vladimir in Russia, is an Upper Palaeolithic site excavated from 1957 by Otto Bader. Its burials, dated to about 34,000-31,000 years ago, include an adult man and two children buried head to head, wearing clothing sewn with more than 13,000 mammoth-ivory beads in total, along with ivory spears, carved animals and fox-tooth belts. Making the beads would have taken thousands of hours. Ancient DNA (Sikora et al., 2017) showed the individuals were not close relatives, suggesting these hunter-gatherers avoided inbreeding through wider social networks.",
 [('Social rank','The rich child burials have been read as evidence of inherited status in Ice Age society; others suggest the children were special for other reasons, such as ritual roles, since one had a skeletal condition.')],
 ['wiki:Sungir','doi:10.1126/science.aao1807'],img='Sungir'),
X('kostenki','Kostenki: early modern humans on the Don',H,'confirmed',3,-45000,['archaeological','genetic (ancient DNA)','radiocarbon'],
 "The Kostenki-Borshchevo sites on the Don River in Russia contain over 20 Upper Palaeolithic sites, some layers dated to around 45,000-42,000 years ago, among the earliest evidence of modern humans on the East European plain. Later layers include mammoth-bone dwellings. The genome of 'Kostenki 14', a man who lived about 37,000 years ago, published in 2014, showed that the main ancestral lineages of later Europeans had already split by then.",
 [('Early arrival','The earliest dates, below a volcanic ash layer from the Campanian Ignimbrite eruption (about 40,000 years ago), are taken by many as evidence of a fast spread of modern humans; some researchers question the cultural attribution of the oldest layers.')],
 ['wiki:Kostyonki–Borshchyovo','doi:10.1126/science.aaa0114'],img='Kostyonki–Borshchyovo'),
X('rurik-varangians','Rurik and the calling of the Varangians',H,'disputed',2,862,['textual','archaeological','genetic'],
 "The Russian Primary Chronicle, compiled in Kyiv around 1113, says that in 862 feuding Slavic and Finnic tribes invited a Varangian (Scandinavian) named Rurik and his brothers to rule them, founding the dynasty that ruled Rus' and later Muscovy until 1598. Archaeology at Staraya Ladoga, Gnezdovo and Novgorod confirms a strong Scandinavian presence in the 9th-10th centuries. Whether Rurik himself existed as described is uncertain, since the chronicle was written 250 years later.",
 [('Normanist vs anti-Normanist','From the 18th century a long, often politicised debate set the Normanist view (Scandinavian founders of the Rus\' state) against anti-Normanists who stressed Slavic origins; most historians now accept a substantial Scandinavian role alongside Slavic, Finnic and Baltic peoples.'),
  ('Identity of Rurik','Some historians have tentatively identified Rurik with Rorik of Dorestad, a Danish Viking known from Frankish sources; this is not proven.')],
 ['wiki:Rurik','wiki:Primary_Chronicle'],img='Rurik'),
X('tsar-bomba','Tsar Bomba, the largest explosion ever made',H,'confirmed',2,1961,['documentary','instrumental'],
 "On 30 October 1961 the Soviet Union detonated the AN602 hydrogen bomb, later nicknamed Tsar Bomba, over Novaya Zemlya in the Arctic. Its yield was about 50 megatons, roughly 1,500 times the Hiroshima and Nagasaki bombs combined; it was designed for 100 megatons but scaled down to reduce fallout. The mushroom cloud rose about 60 km and windows broke hundreds of kilometres away. It was a demonstration and was never meant as a practical weapon.",
 [('Propaganda value','Historians generally see the test as a political display during the Berlin crisis rather than a military step; the full yield figures were confirmed after the Soviet era.')],
 ['wiki:Tsar_Bomba'],img='Tsar_Bomba'),
X('petrozavodsk-1977','Petrozavodsk phenomenon',H,'disputed',3,1977,['eyewitness','documentary'],
 "Early on 20 September 1977 residents of Petrozavodsk in Soviet Karelia and observers across northern Europe saw a glowing 'jellyfish' shaped object with rays of light. Soviet press reports led to an official study (programme 'Setka'). Researchers, including Yuli Platov and others in the Soviet Academy of Sciences, attributed it to the launch of the secret Kosmos-955 satellite from the Plesetsk cosmodrome, whose rocket exhaust lit by the sun at high altitude produced the effect.",
 [('Rocket explanation','The rocket launch explanation is accepted by most researchers and matches the timing; similar lights were later seen after other Plesetsk launches.'),
  ('Unexplained','Some UFO researchers argue that not all reports, such as claims of holes in windows, are explained by a launch; these claims are not verified.')],
 ['wiki:Petrozavodsk_phenomenon'],img='Petrozavodsk_phenomenon'),
X('lake-vostok','Lake Vostok: a lake sealed under Antarctic ice',H,'confirmed',3,1996,['instrumental','glaciological'],
 "Lake Vostok lies about 4 km beneath the ice near Russia's Vostok Station in Antarctica and is the largest known subglacial lake, about 250 km long. It was suggested by Russian geographer Andrey Kapitsa from seismic soundings in the 1950s-60s and confirmed by radar and satellite data in 1996. It may have been isolated for millions of years. Russian drilling reached the lake water in 2012. Analyses of refrozen water have reported possible DNA traces of microbes, but contamination from drilling fluid makes these results disputed.",
 [('Life in the lake','Some studies (e.g. 2013) reported thousands of gene sequences suggesting a diverse ecosystem; critics argue these mostly reflect contamination. It is also studied as an analogue for ice-covered moons such as Europa.')],
 ['wiki:Lake_Vostok'],img='Lake_Vostok'),
X('qalupalik','Qalupalik, the Inuit sea creature',M,'myth-legend',3,1000,['oral tradition'],
 "In Inuit oral tradition the qalupalik is a human-like creature with green skin and long hair that lives in the sea and snatches children who wander too close to the water's edge or the ice, carrying them off in a pouch or amauti on its back. Its humming may be heard before it appears. Folklorists note that such stories also served to keep children away from dangerous sea ice. The tradition is old; the date shown is a placeholder.",
 [('Function','Scholars generally interpret the qalupalik as a cautionary tale, while for many Inuit it is also part of a living spiritual world of sea beings.')],
 ['wiki:Qalupalik'],dateNote='Traditional; date is a placeholder for Inuit oral tradition'),
X('kushtaka','Kushtaka, the land-otter people',M,'myth-legend',3,1000,['oral tradition'],
 "In the traditions of the Tlingit and Tsimshian peoples of south-east Alaska and British Columbia, the kushtaka ('land otter man') is a shapeshifting being that can take human form, lure people (especially those lost or drowning at sea) by imitating the cries of babies or relatives, and turn them into kushtaka. Shamans were said to be able to rescue victims.",
 [('Interpretations','Anthropologists link the stories to the real dangers of drowning and exposure; some cryptid writers have connected them to Bigfoot-like creatures, which is speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Kushtaka'],dateNote='Traditional; date is a placeholder'),
X('raven-tradition','Raven steals the light',M,'myth-legend',3,1000,['oral tradition'],
 "Across the North Pacific coast, among the Tlingit, Haida, Tsimshian and others, the trickster and creator Raven is central to oral tradition. In a widespread story the world is dark because a chief keeps the sun, moon and stars in boxes; Raven becomes a hemlock needle, is swallowed by the chief's daughter, is reborn as her son, and as a spoiled child persuades his grandfather to let him play with the boxes, releasing the light. Related Raven stories are told in Siberia among the Chukchi and Koryak.",
 [('Transpacific links','Shared Raven stories in Siberia and North-West America have been read by some folklorists as evidence of very old connections across the Bering region; others see parallel development.')],
 ['wiki:Ravens_in_Native_American_mythology'],img='Ravens_in_Native_American_mythology',dateNote='Traditional; date is a placeholder'),
X('ozette-village','Ozette: a village buried by a mudslide',H,'confirmed',3,1560,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Ozette, a Makah village on the Olympic Peninsula coast of Washington State, was partly engulfed by a mudslide, dated by radiocarbon to around 1560 (other accounts give around 1750). Waterlogged clay preserved collapsed houses and their contents, giving an unusually complete picture of a whaling and fishing community before abandonment or looting: tens of thousands of objects such as baskets, harpoons, weaving gear and a carved cedar whale fin inlaid with sea-otter teeth. After storm erosion exposed the site, it was excavated from 1970 to 1981 by the Makah together with Washington State University archaeologists. Finds are kept at the Makah Museum in Neah Bay.",
 [('Dating','The exact date of the slide differs between sources, and more than one slide may have affected the village over time.')],
 ['wiki:Ozette_Indian_Village_Archeological_Site','wiki:Makah_Museum'],img='Ozette_Indian_Village_Archeological_Site',dateNote='Radiocarbon c. 1560; some sources say c. 1750'),
]
# --- part 3: Japan and Mesoamerica
E+=[
X('sannai-maruyama','Sannai-Maruyama: a large Jomon village',H,'confirmed',3,-3900,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "Sannai-Maruyama in Aomori, northern Japan, was a settlement of Jomon hunter-gatherers occupied from about 3900 to 2200 BCE. Excavations from 1992, when the site was due to become a baseball stadium, revealed hundreds of pit dwellings, large buildings, storehouses, burial areas, thousands of clay figurines (dogu) and the post holes of a structure built on six huge chestnut posts. Evidence suggests chestnut trees were managed. It is part of the UNESCO World Heritage 'Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan' (2021).",
 [('Complexity','The size and long occupation challenge the old idea that complex settled communities require farming; how many people lived there at once is debated.'),
  ('Six-pillar structure','Its purpose is unknown; proposals include a watchtower, a shrine or a platform aligned with the solstices, and the reconstruction on site is a hypothesis.')],
 ['wiki:Sannai-Maruyama_site','url:https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1632/|Jomon Prehistoric Sites in Northern Japan (UNESCO)|heritage'],img='Sannai-Maruyama_site'),
X('kamikaze-typhoons','The kamikaze: divine winds against the Mongol fleets',H,'confirmed',2,1274,['textual','archaeological (underwater)'],
 "Kublai Khan's Mongol-led Yuan dynasty attempted to invade Japan in 1274 and 1281. Both times storms struck the fleet: the 1281 fleet, reportedly with over 4,000 ships, was destroyed by a typhoon off Takashima. The Japanese came to call these 'kamikaze' (divine winds), sent in answer to prayers at shrines such as Ise. Underwater archaeology at Takashima from the 1980s has found wrecks, anchors, weapons and early explosive bombs (tetsuhau); study suggested many ships were hastily built river boats unsuited to the open sea.",
 [('Role of the storms','Some historians stress the samurai defence and the coastal wall at Hakata as much as the storms; the 1274 storm is less certain in sources than that of 1281.'),
  ('Legacy','The idea of divine protection was later used in nationalist ideology and gave its name to the suicide pilots of 1944-45.')],
 ['wiki:Kamikaze_(typhoon)','wiki:Mongol_invasions_of_Japan'],img='Mongol_invasions_of_Japan',end=1281),
X('masakado-curse',"Taira no Masakado's head and the Tokyo curse",M,'myth-legend',3,940,['textual','folklore'],
 "Taira no Masakado was a 10th-century warrior who rebelled against the court in Kyoto and declared himself 'new emperor'. He was killed in 940 and his head was displayed in Kyoto. Legend says the head flew back east to his home region and fell at Shibasaki village, now in central Tokyo, where the Masakado Kubizuka (head mound) still stands among office towers in Otemachi. Popular stories blame misfortunes on his vengeful spirit whenever the mound has been disturbed or threatened by redevelopment, and the site is still carefully maintained and visited.",
 [('Historical Masakado','His revolt is recorded in the Shomonki chronicle; he later became a folk hero and was enshrined at Kanda Myojin.'),
  ('The curse','The curse stories are folklore; no evidence links disturbance of the mound to the misfortunes, though many people treat the site with respect.')],
 ['wiki:Taira_no_Masakado'],img='Taira_no_Masakado'),
X('tsuchinoko','Tsuchinoko, the fat snake of Japan',M,'myth-legend',3,1700,['folklore','eyewitness'],
 "The tsuchinoko is a cryptid of Japanese folklore described as a thick-bodied snake, sometimes able to jump or roll like a hoop, appearing in Edo-period sources. Sightings boomed in the 1970s and several municipalities and companies have offered rewards for a captured specimen. None has ever been confirmed. Sceptics suggest sightings may be of known snakes that have just eaten a large meal, or of other misidentified reptiles.",
 [('Undiscovered snake','Some enthusiasts argue an unknown species of viper may exist in rural Japan; no specimen supports this.')],
 ['wiki:Tsuchinoko'],img='Tsuchinoko',dateNote='Edo-period sources; modern sighting boom in the 1970s'),
X('kappa','Kappa, the water imp',M,'myth-legend',3,1700,['folklore','textual'],
 "The kappa is a water spirit (yokai) of Japanese folklore, child-sized with a beak, a shell and a dish-like hollow on its head holding water that gives it strength. It was said to drown people and horses and pull out the shirikodama, a mythical ball in the anus, but also to be polite and to keep promises. Mummified 'kappa' relics kept in temples have been found to be composites or animal remains. Some scholars link kappa stories to drowning deaths and to the bodies of children in rivers.",
 [('Origins','Folklorists see the kappa as an Edo-period merging of earlier river deities and Chinese water creatures; some writers have speculated about aliens or unknown animals, which is unsupported.')],
 ['wiki:Kappa_(folklore)'],img='Kappa_(folklore)'),
X('bonampak-murals','Bonampak murals',H,'confirmed',3,791,['archaeological','iconographic'],
 "In 1946 Giles Healey and the Lacandon Maya brought to outside attention three painted rooms in a small temple at Bonampak, Chiapas, Mexico, painted around 790 CE. The murals show a royal court, a battle, captives being tortured and celebrations, overturning the 1940s view that the Classic Maya were a peaceful society of priest-astronomers.",
 [('Interpretation','The murals were long read as straightforward history; recent scholars note they also served royal propaganda shortly before the site was abandoned.')],
 ['wiki:Bonampak'],img='Bonampak',changed="Before 1946 many scholars considered the Maya peaceful; Bonampak and the decipherment of Maya glyphs showed frequent warfare."),
X('mesoamerican-ballgame','The Mesoamerican ballgame',H,'confirmed',2,-1650,['archaeological','textual','iconographic'],
 "A ritual team game played with a heavy solid rubber ball was played across Mesoamerica for over 3,000 years. The oldest known court, at Paso de la Amada in Chiapas, dates to about 1650 BCE, and rubber balls from El Manati date to around 1600 BCE. In some contexts the game was linked to cosmology and human sacrifice; in the K'iche' Maya Popol Vuh the Hero Twins play ball against the lords of the underworld, Xibalba. A version called ulama is still played in Sinaloa.",
 [('Who was sacrificed','Popular accounts say the winners were sacrificed; most scholars think captives or losers were, and that practice varied greatly over time and place.')],
 ['wiki:Mesoamerican_ballgame'],img='Mesoamerican_ballgame'),
X('huey-tzompantli','The Huey Tzompantli: the Aztec skull rack',H,'confirmed',3,1486,['archaeological','textual'],
 "Spanish conquistadors described a great rack of skulls (tzompantli) in front of the Templo Mayor in Tenochtitlan. From 2015, archaeologists of Mexico's INAH excavated part of the Huey Tzompantli in Mexico City, finding a rack and towers of skulls set in mortar, with hundreds of skulls of men, women and children. The finds confirm the scale of Aztec sacrifice described in Spanish and Indigenous sources, though they also show that victims were not only adult male warriors.",
 [('Numbers','Older accounts claimed 130,000 skulls; archaeologists caution that the full count is unknown and the Spanish had reasons to exaggerate.')],
 ['wiki:Tzompantli'],img='Tzompantli',dateNote='Main phases built c. 1486-1502'),
X('codex-zouche-nuttall','Codex Zouche-Nuttall and the Mixtec lord Eight Deer',H,'confirmed',3,1063,['textual','iconographic'],
 "The Codex Zouche-Nuttall is one of a few surviving pre-Hispanic Mixtec screenfold books, painted on deerskin and now in the British Museum. It records genealogies and the life of the 11th-century ruler Eight Deer Jaguar Claw (1063-1115), who expanded his power through conquest and alliance and was eventually sacrificed. Many Mixtec codices were destroyed after the Spanish conquest, making this one of the few Indigenous historical books from the Americas.",
 [('History or myth','Scholars debate how far the codices record real events and how far they blend royal history with sacred and mythic time.')],
 ['wiki:Codex_Zouche-Nuttall','wiki:Eight_Deer_Jaguar_Claw'],img='Codex_Zouche-Nuttall',end=1115),
X('hachiko-statue','Hachiko, the loyal dog',H,'confirmed',3,1925,['documentary','photographic'],
 "Hachiko was an Akita dog who met his owner, Professor Hidesaburo Ueno, at Shibuya Station in Tokyo each day. After Ueno died suddenly in May 1925, Hachiko kept returning to the station for almost ten years until his death in 1935. His story became famous after a 1932 newspaper article and a statue was put up in 1934. Scientists studied his remains in 2011 and found terminal cancer and a filarial worm infection.",
 [('Why he waited','Some writers suggest that vendors at the station feeding him was one reason he kept coming; this does not alter the documented story of his long vigil.')],
 ['wiki:Hachikō'],img='Hachikō',end=1935),
]
# --- part 4: official UFO investigations, scientific hoaxes and anomalies
E+=[
X('lubbock-lights','Lubbock Lights',H,'disputed',3,1951,['eyewitness','photographic'],
 "In August-September 1951 several professors at Texas Technological College in Lubbock, and many other residents, reported arcs of faint lights flying silently overhead in formation. Student Carl Hart Jr. took photographs of a V-shaped group of lights. The US Air Force's Project Blue Book investigated; its head Edward Ruppelt wrote that the professors' sightings were probably birds (plovers) reflecting street lights, while the photographs were never fully explained.",
 [('Birds','The bird explanation is widely accepted by sceptics; some witnesses rejected it.'),
  ('Unexplained','UFO researchers regard the case as one of the classic unexplained sightings, particularly the Hart photographs.')],
 ['wiki:Lubbock_Lights'],img='Lubbock_Lights'),
X('washington-1952','1952 Washington, D.C. UFO incident',H,'disputed',2,1952,['radar','eyewitness','documentary'],
 "On the weekends of 19-20 and 26-27 July 1952 air traffic controllers at Washington National Airport and Andrews Air Force Base tracked unidentified radar targets near the capital, and pilots and ground observers reported lights. Jet interceptors were sent up. Headlines nationwide led to the largest Pentagon press conference since the Second World War, where General John Samford attributed the targets to temperature inversions causing false radar returns. The event contributed to the CIA's 1953 Robertson Panel.",
 [('Weather explanation','The Air Force and later the Condon Report favoured temperature inversion and misidentified stars.'),
  ('Disputed explanation','Some controllers and later researchers argued the targets did not behave like inversion effects and that visual sightings matched them.')],
 ['wiki:1952_Washington,_D.C._UFO_incident','wiki:Robertson_Panel'],img='1952_Washington,_D.C._UFO_incident'),
X('condon-report','The Condon Report',H,'confirmed',2,1968,['documentary','scientific study'],
 "From 1966 to 1968 the University of Colorado, funded by the US Air Force, studied UFO reports under physicist Edward Condon. The final report, published in 1969, concluded that nothing had come from UFO studies in the past 21 years that had added to scientific knowledge and that further extensive study was probably not justified. A National Academy of Sciences panel endorsed its methods, and Project Blue Book was closed in December 1969. About 30% of the cases studied were left unexplained.",
 [('Criticism','Critics, including astronomer J. Allen Hynek and physicist James McDonald, argued that Condon had decided his conclusions in advance and pointed to the \'Low memo\' suggesting the project would look objective while finding nothing; defenders argue the case studies speak for themselves.')],
 ['wiki:Condon_Committee'],img='Condon_Committee'),
X('majestic-12','Majestic 12 documents',H,'debunked',3,1984,['documentary','forensic'],
 "In 1984 TV producer Jaime Shandera received a roll of film showing a supposed 1952 briefing for President-elect Eisenhower about 'Operation Majestic Twelve', a secret group said to have recovered alien craft and bodies at Roswell. The FBI investigated and described the documents as 'completely bogus', and analysts found anachronistic date formats, a copied Truman signature and other problems. They are generally considered forgeries, though some UFO researchers still debate them.",
 [('Defenders','Researchers such as Stanton Friedman argued the documents could be genuine; the US Air Force and most investigators regard them as fakes.'),
  ('Disinformation','Some have suggested the documents were planted by intelligence officers as disinformation; this is speculative.')],
 ['wiki:Majestic_12'],img='Majestic_12'),
X('ummo-affair','The UMMO affair',H,'debunked',3,1966,['documentary','testimony','photographic'],
 "From 1966, beginning in Madrid, Spanish and later French UFO enthusiasts and esoteric groups received long typewritten letters claiming to come from aliens from the planet Ummo, said to orbit a star in Virgo, covering physics, biology and philosophy. Photographs of a disc bearing the same symbol, supposedly taken at San José de Valderas near Madrid on 1 June 1967, were circulated. José Luis Jordán Peña, who had himself reported seeing a craft with the symbol in 1966, later claimed responsibility for writing the letters, and most investigators regard the affair as an elaborate hoax; who else was involved is unknown.",
 [('Hoax','Most researchers accept that the letters were written on Earth, probably by Jordán Peña and possibly others; Jacques Vallée compared the authors to Borges\' fictional inventors of a world.'),
  ('Believers','French physicist Jean-Pierre Petit has argued since 1991 that some letters show superior scientific ideas, and small groups of followers remain.')],
 ['wiki:UMMO_affair'],img='UMMO_affair'),
X('clever-hans','Clever Hans, the horse who could count',H,'debunked',3,1904,['experimental','documentary'],
 "In early 1900s Berlin a horse called Hans, owned by Wilhelm von Osten, appeared to do arithmetic, tell time and spell by tapping his hoof. A 1904 commission found no trickery, but in 1907 psychologist Oskar Pfungst showed that Hans was responding to tiny unconscious cues in the posture and face of the questioner, and could not answer when the questioner did not know the answer. The 'Clever Hans effect' became a key reason for blind testing in psychology and animal cognition research.",
 [('Animal intelligence','The case is sometimes used to dismiss animal intelligence; researchers note that Hans was remarkably skilled at reading human cues, which is a form of intelligence in itself.')],
 ['wiki:Clever_Hans'],img='Clever_Hans'),
X('sokal-affair','The Sokal affair',H,'confirmed',3,1996,['documentary'],
 "In 1996 physicist Alan Sokal had a deliberately nonsensical article, arguing that quantum gravity is a social construct, published in the cultural studies journal Social Text, then revealed it as a hoax to show weak scrutiny of postmodernist work. The episode fed wider 'science wars' debates. A 2017-18 'grievance studies' affair by Peter Boghossian, James Lindsay and Helen Pluckrose repeated the approach with several journals.",
 [('Interpretation','Supporters see it as exposing lack of rigour in some humanities scholarship; critics see it as an ethically questionable stunt that proved little about whole fields.')],
 ['wiki:Sokal_affair'],img='Sokal_affair'),
X('archaeoraptor','Archaeoraptor: the fake feathered dinosaur',H,'debunked',3,1999,['fossil','CT scan'],
 "In November 1999 National Geographic announced 'Archaeoraptor liaoningensis' from China as a missing link between dinosaurs and birds. It turned out to be a composite: a farmer had glued together a bird's body and the tail of a small dromaeosaur dinosaur to make it more valuable. CT scans in 2000-2001 confirmed the forgery. The parts were later identified as the bird Yanornis and the dinosaur Microraptor, which itself turned out to be a genuine feathered dinosaur.",
 [('Lesson','The episode is often cited in debates on the fossil trade and on peer review before publicity; it did not weaken the evidence for the dinosaur origin of birds, which rests on many genuine fossils.')],
 ['wiki:Archaeoraptor'],img='Archaeoraptor'),
X('tully-monster','The Tully monster',H,'disputed',3,-307000000,['fossil'],
 "Tullimonstrum gregarium is a soft-bodied animal about 30 cm long from the 307-million-year-old Mazon Creek fossil beds in Illinois, first collected by amateur Francis Tully in 1955. It had a long proboscis ending in a toothed claw and eyes on a bar. Its classification has been a long puzzle: a 2016 study proposed it was a vertebrate related to lampreys, while later studies (2017, 2023) argued it was not a vertebrate. It is the state fossil of Illinois.",
 [('Cryptozoology','Some cryptozoologists have linked the Tully monster to lake-monster sightings; it is known only from fossils over 300 million years old, and there is no evidence it survived.')],
 ['wiki:Tullimonstrum','doi:10.1038/nature16992'],img='Tullimonstrum'),
X('betz-sphere','The Betz mystery sphere',H,'debunked',3,1974,['eyewitness','documentary'],
 "In 1974 the Betz family found a polished metal sphere about 20 cm across and weighing about 10 kg near Jacksonville, Florida. It appeared to roll by itself and the press speculated about alien or spy technology. In April 1974 the US Navy said it was simply a large stainless-steel ball bearing of the kind used as a check valve in industrial piping; the rolling was attributed to the uneven floors of the old house. Later, an artist reported having lost several such check-valve balls from his van's roof rack near Jacksonville in 1971.",
 [('Unexplained','The family disputed the Navy verdict at the time and some UFO literature still lists the sphere as an anomaly; investigators regard it as an ordinary industrial ball.')],
 ['wiki:Betz_mystery_sphere']),
]
